[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional torus T^n is necessarily abelian.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA Lie group G homeomorphic to T^n = (S^1)^n. We need to determine if G must be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mabelian.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- T^n is compact, connected, abelian Lie group (the standard one).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- A Lie group homeomorphic to T^n is compact and connected.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- The fundamental group of T^n is Z^n.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- For a connected Lie group G, π_1(G) is abelian and isomorphic to the kernel of[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe universal cover.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet G be a connected compact Lie group with π_1(G) ≅ Z^n (since homeomorphic to [0m[38;2;124;124;124mT^n).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe universal cover of G is a simply connected compact Lie group... wait, not [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnecessarily. The universal cover of a compact Lie group need not be compact. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the universal cover of a compact Lie group is compact iff π_1 is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfinite.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHere π_1(G) ≅ Z^n is infinite (for n ≥ 1), so the universal cover is non-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think differently. G is compact, connected. Its Lie algebra g is compact [0m[38;2;124;124;124m(admits an invariant inner product). The universal cover G~ is a simply [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconnected Lie group with Lie algebra g.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince g is a compact Lie algebra, g = z ⊕ [g,g] where z is the center and [g,g] [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= g_ss is semisimple compact. The simply connected group with Lie algebra g is Z[0m[38;2;124;124;124m× G_ss where Z is the simply connected group with Lie algebra z (a vector space [0m[38;2;124;124;124mR^k) and G_ss is the simply connected compact semisimple group.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo G~ ≅ R^k × G_ss where G_ss is simply connected compact semisimple.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow G = G~/Γ where Γ is a discrete central subgroup isomorphic to π_1(G) ≅ Z^n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mΓ ⊂ Z(G~) = R^k × Z(G_ss). Note Z(G_ss) is finite (since G_ss is compact [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msemisimple, its center is finite).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo Γ is a discrete subgroup of R^k × F where F = Z(G_ss) is finite. Since Γ ≅ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mZ^n is torsion-free, the projection of Γ to F must be trivial (any element with [0m[38;2;124;124;124mnontrivial finite part would have finite order... actually need to be careful). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think: Γ is a subgroup of R^k × F. The projection π: R^k × F → F. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mkernel of π is Γ ∩ (R^k × {e}) which is a subgroup of R^k. The image π(Γ) is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msubgroup of F, hence finite. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mΓ/(Γ ∩ R^k) ≅ π(Γ) is finite. Since Γ ≅ Z^n is torsion-free, can it have a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfinite quotient that's nontrivial? Yes, Z^n can have finite quotients. But the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124missue is whether Γ being torsion-free forces π(Γ) = {e}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider an element γ ∈ Γ with nontrivial projection to F. Then γ = (v, f) with [0m[38;2;124;124;124mf ≠ e, f of finite order m. Then γ^m = (mv, e) ∈ R^k. So γ has infinite order [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(good, consistent with torsion-free). But γ itself is fine.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, so Γ could potentially have elements projecting nontrivially to F. Let me [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mreconsider.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually wait. Let's think about the topology. G = G~/Γ is homeomorphic to T^n. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mG~ = R^k × G_ss. G_ss is a simply connected compact semisimple Lie group. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mG = (R^k × G_ss)/Γ where Γ ≅ Z^n acts freely and properly discontinuously, and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe quotient is compact (homeomorphic to T^n).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the quotient to be compact, Γ must be a cocompact lattice in G~. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow G_ss is compact, so the projection of Γ to R^k must be a lattice (cocompact)[0m[38;2;124;124;124min R^k, otherwise the R^k factor wouldn't be compactified. So the projection p: [0m[38;2;124;124;124mΓ → R^k has image a lattice Λ ≅ Z^k in R^k (full rank k). This requires k ≤ n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe kernel of p: Γ → R^k is Γ ∩ ({0} × G_ss) = Γ ∩ G_ss, a subgroup of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcenter of G_ss (finite). Since Γ is torsion-free, this kernel is trivial. So Γ [0m
